WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.480
After a nerve injury, pain can outlast the injury because the nervous system itself

2
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.460
changes. Sensory neurons can fire without a stimulus.

3
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.700
The spinal cord receives more excitation.

4
00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.680
Immune cells can sustain the sensitivity.

5
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.380
These changes matter because persistent pain can reflect altered processing at

6
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.260
several levels. It is more than an acute warning that keeps going.

7
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.260
Each experiment measures a particular part of that change.

8
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.690
Start with the first neuron.

9
00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:40.430
Cultured human sensory neurons from tumour surgery sometimes fired spontaneously.

10
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:46.030
That firing was associated with the donors' radicular or neuropathic pain.

11
00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.570
This is an association in a dish.

12
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:54.550
Rat studies also show spontaneous firing in uninjured neighbours after nerve injury.

13
00:00:55.430 --> 00:01:00.330
In other rat recordings, spinal neurons received more frequent excitatory synaptic

14
00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.210
events. That measures input to a neuron.

15
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.670
It does not measure unpleasantness.

16
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.410
Next, immune cells can change neuronal signalling.

17
00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:16.270
In nerve-injured rats, lowering P2X4 receptors on spinal microglia reduced touch

18
00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:20.510
hypersensitivity. A separate rat study supplied a link to neurons.

19
00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.870
Microglia stimulated with ATP released BDNF.

20
00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:29.650
That shifted the anion reversal potential of lamina I neurons.

21
00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:35.570
Blocking BDNF signalling through TrkB reversed both the shift and the

22
00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:39.110
hypersensitivity. These are links in a pathway, in rats.

23
00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:45.870
Now the brain. Brain circuits shape the unpleasant side of pain, and they also send

24
00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:46.930
signals back down.

25
00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:53.170
In rodent experiments, stimulating the anterior cingulate cortex strengthened spinal

26
00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:54.450
excitatory transmission.

27
00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.090
Descending control can facilitate signalling too.

28
00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:00.090
Keep the measurements apart.

29
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.670
A withdrawal reflex is not the whole pain experience.

30
00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:08.270
More synaptic events cannot tell us how unpleasant a stimulus feels.

31
00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:12.150
Finally, the immune route depends on the context.

32
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:18.310
In the tested mice, a microglial inhibitor reversed hypersensitivity in males, but

33
00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:22.270
not females. Females appeared to use adaptive immune cells.

34
00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.130
Without those cells, the inhibitor worked.

35
00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.030
The route could switch.

36
00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:28.970
Compartment matters too.

37
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:34.090
Depleting macrophages reduced hypersensitivity in both sexes of mice.

38
00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:39.510
The authors favoured ganglion macrophages, but could not exclude skin dendritic

39
00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:44.770
cells. These animal findings do not establish a fixed rule for men and women.
